1. What is CI/CD?

First, understand two important terms.

CI – Continuous Integration

Continuous Integration means frequently combining code changes from different developers into a shared repository and automatically building and testing the code.

CD – Continuous Delivery / Continuous Deployment

CD refers to automating the process of preparing or deploying software after it has been integrated and tested.

In simple words:

* CI = Automatically integrate and test code.
* CD = Automatically prepare or deploy the application.

Real-world example

Imagine 5 developers are working on the same website.

* Developer A changes the login page.
* Developer B changes the registration page.
* Developer C changes the homepage.
* All developers upload their changes to GitHub.
* Jenkins automatically detects changes.
* Jenkins downloads the latest code.
* Jenkins builds and tests the application.

This is the basic idea behind CI/CD.

2. What is a Build Pipeline?

A build pipeline is a sequence of automated tasks that execute one after another.

For example:

Source Code

Developer writes code.

GitHub

Code is uploaded.

Jenkins

Detects changes and starts the build.

Build and Test

Checks whether the code works.

Success

Build completes successfully.

