4. Part A – Integrating Jenkins with GitHub

Let’s understand the practical steps.

Step 1: Create a GitHub repository

* Open GitHub.
* Create a new repository.
* Add a file such as index.html.
* Upload the file.

Step 2: Open Jenkins

Open your Jenkins dashboard.

Click:

New Item → Enter project name → Freestyle Project → OK

Step 3: Configure GitHub Project

In the Jenkins configuration page:

Go to General → GitHub project.

Enter your GitHub project URL.

For example:

https://github.com/username/integration/

This tells Jenkins which GitHub project is associated with the job.

Step 4: Configure Source Code Management

Find the Source Code Management section.

Select Git.

Under Repository URL, enter:

https://github.com/username/integration.git

This tells Jenkins where to download the source code from.

Step 5: Select the branch

Under Branches to Build, enter:

*/main

This tells Jenkins to use the main branch.

Step 6: Select Repository Browser

Select:

githubweb

This identifies GitHub as the repository browser.

Step 7: Configure Build Triggers

Find the Build Triggers section.

Select:

GitHub hook trigger for GITScm polling

What does this do?

It allows Jenkins to receive GitHub webhook notifications when code changes occur.

A webhook is an automatic notification sent from one application to another when an event happens.

For example:

When a developer pushes new code to GitHub, GitHub sends a notification to Jenkins.

Jenkins can then start a build.

Step 8: Save and Build

Click Save.

Then click Build Now.

Open:

Build History → Build Number → Console Output

If everything is configured correctly, you should see:

Finished: SUCCESS

Part A completed

5. Part B – Creating a Build Pipeline

Now comes the second part.

Your lab record asks you to create three Jenkins jobs:

* Job 1
* Job 2
* Job 3

Step 1: Create three jobs

Go to Jenkins Dashboard.

Click:

New Item → Enter job name → Freestyle Project → OK

Create the following three jobs:

Job1
Job2
Job3

You can create them one by one.

Step 2: Install Build Pipeline Plugin

The Build Pipeline plugin is used to visualize the execution of Jenkins jobs in a pipeline.

Follow these steps:

* Open Manage Jenkins.
* Select Plugins.
* Open Available Plugins.
* Search for Build Pipeline.
* Install the plugin.

Step 3: Create a Build Pipeline View

After installing the plugin:

* Go to Jenkins Dashboard.
* Click the + icon near the views.
* Enter a name, for example My Pipeline.
* Select Build Pipeline View.
* Click Create.

Step 4: Select Initial Job

You will see an option called:

Select Initial Job

Choose:

Job1

This means Job1 is the starting job of your pipeline.

Click Apply → OK.

Step 5: Run the Pipeline

Click Run.

Jenkins will display the pipeline view.

Your lab record demonstrates a green successful build in the pipeline.

Important clarification: Creating three jobs and selecting Job1 as the initial job creates the pipeline view. To execute Job2 and Job3 automatically in sequence, you must also configure downstream build triggers between the jobs. The record does not describe those additional configurations.

